\title{The Splitting-Type Channel of a Number Field\\ Is Exactly Its Abelianization Content}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $K$ be the splitting field of a monic irreducible $f \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$ of degree $n$, with Galois group $G \le S_n$ acting on the roots. For an unramified prime $p$, the \emph{splitting type} $T(p)$ is the multiset of degrees of the irreducible factors of $f \bmod p$, equivalently the cycle type of the Frobenius class. We measure, in bits, the information that the residue class of $p$ carries about $T(p)$, and prove that it is exactly the abelianization content of $G$: writing $G' = [G,G]$ and $m^{*}$ for the conductor of the abelian characters of $G$,
\[
I\big(p \bmod m^{*};\,T\big) \;=\; I\big(\Frob_p G';\,T\big) \;=\; H(T) - H(T \mid \text{coset}) \;\le\; \log_2 [G:G'] .
\]
We prove a determinism criterion for conditional entropy and deduce a \emph{completeness criterion}: the channel attains the cap $\log_2[G:G']$ if and only if the splitting type determines the abelianization coset. Abelian groups deliver the full type entropy $H(T)$ for every readout; perfect groups deliver exactly $0$. We evaluate the channel in closed form for six fields, with Galois groups $S_3$, $S_4$, $A_4$, $D_4$, $V_4$, $C_4$, at the prime level and at the semiprime level $N = pq$; every value is an exact expression in $\log_2 3$ and $\log_2 5$. Three structural phenomena are isolated: the cap is a statement about the abelianization and not about the number of types (the five-type field $x^4-x-1$ carries $\tfrac32 + \tfrac38\log_2 3 \approx 2.09436$ bits of splitting entropy and leaks exactly one); the \emph{reversal}, in which the non-abelian $D_4$ channel strictly exceeds the abelian $V_4$ channel; and the \emph{which-factor wall}, by which disclosing which prime factor of a semiprime carried which splitting shape is worth exactly zero bits. Measurement over the primes below $6\cdot 10^{4}$ reproduces every closed form to within finite-sample noise.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} splitting type, Frobenius, Chebotarev density, abelianization, commutator subgroup, mutual information, class field theory, side-channel capacity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Fix a monic irreducible $f \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$ of degree $n$ and a prime $p \nmid \disc(f)$. The reduction $f \bmod p$ factors into distinct irreducibles over $\mathbb{F}_p$, and the multiset of their degrees --- the \emph{splitting type} $T(p)$ --- is a coarse but fundamental arithmetic invariant. For $n=3$ the possible types are $[1,1,1]$, $[2,1]$, $[3]$; for $n=4$ they are $[1,1,1,1]$, $[2,1,1]$, $[2,2]$, $[3,1]$, $[4]$.

For $f = x^2+1$ the splitting type is governed by a congruence: $f$ splits precisely when $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$. This is the paradigm of reciprocity, available exactly when the Galois group of the splitting field is abelian. For non-abelian Galois groups no congruence determines the splitting type. The classical statement of this fact is qualitative; the purpose of this paper is to make it quantitative and exact. We ask: how many bits about the splitting type does the residue class of $p$ carry?

\subsection{Statement of the main result}

Let $G \le S_n$ be the Galois group of the splitting field of $f$, let $G'=[G,G]$ be its commutator subgroup, $G^{\ab} = G/G'$ its abelianization, and $m^{*}$ the conductor of the abelian characters of $G$, i.e.\ the modulus of the maximal abelian subextension.

\begin{maintheorem}[The Type-Channel Law]
\[
I\big(p \bmod m^{*};\,T(p)\big) \;=\; I\big(\Frob_p G';\,T(p)\big) \;=\; H(T) - H\big(T \mid \Frob_p G'\big) \;\le\; \log_2[G:G'],
\]
with equality in the bound if and only if the splitting type determines the abelianization coset.
\end{maintheorem}

The reduction to a finite group computation uses two classical inputs, which supply all the arithmetic in the paper.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Chebotarev (1922).} As $p$ ranges over the unramified primes, $\Frob_p$ is equidistributed over the conjugacy classes of $G$. Since the splitting type is a class function (Lemma~\ref{lem:classfn}), the joint statistics of $(\Frob_p, T(p))$ are those of $(g, T(g))$ for $g$ uniform in $G$.
\item \textbf{Class field theory.} The residue class of an unramified $p$ modulo $m^{*}$ determines, and is determined by, the coset $\Frob_p G' \in G^{\ab}$.
\end{itemize}
Everything downstream is therefore a computation with the uniform measure on a finite group, and each channel evaluated in Section~\ref{sec:prime} is an exact real number.

\subsection{What is new}

Three consequences deserve separate emphasis.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{The cap does not see the type count.} For $G = S_4$ the splitting-type entropy exceeds two bits across five types, but $[S_4:A_4]=2$ caps the channel at one bit --- and the bound is attained.
\item \emph{The reversal.} Abelianness is not what makes a channel rich: the non-abelian $D_4$ channel exceeds the abelian $V_4$ channel by a factor greater than two, at both levels. What matters is how sharply the type readout separates the cosets.
\item \emph{Perfect groups are silent.} If $G'=G$ the channel vanishes identically, for every readout. The smallest instance is $A_5$: the obstruction to solving the quintic in radicals is precisely the obstruction to any congruence-based knowledge of its splitting statistics.
\end{enumerate}

\section{A finite channel calculus}

\begin{definition}[Readouts, probability, entropy]
Let $S$ be a finite nonempty set with the uniform measure. A \emph{readout} is a function $f : S \to A$. Write
\[
\Pr[f=a] = \frac{\#\{w \in S : f(w)=a\}}{\#S}, \qquad
H(f) = -\!\!\sum_{a \in f(S)}\!\! \Pr[f=a]\log_2\Pr[f=a].
\]
For readouts $f : S \to A$ and $g : S \to B$ the joint readout is $w \mapsto (f(w),g(w))$, and
\[
H(g \mid f) = H(f,g) - H(f), \qquad I(f;g) = H(f) + H(g) - H(f,g).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity and monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
$H(f) \ge 0$; $H(f) \le H(f,g)$ and $H(g) \le H(f,g)$; hence $H(g\mid f) \ge 0$ and $I(f;g) \le \min\{H(f),H(g)\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity is termwise. For monotonicity, expand $H(f)$ over the cells of the joint table using $\sum_b \Pr[f=a,g=b] = \Pr[f=a]$ and compare cellwise: for $0 \le q \le r$ with $r>0$, $-q\log_2 r \le -q\log_2 q$. Summing gives $H(f) \le H(f,g)$; symmetry gives the other inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Gibbs]\label{lem:gibbs}
$I(f;g) \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $P(a,b)=\Pr[f=a,g=b]$ and $Q(a,b)=\Pr[f=a]\Pr[g=b]$ one has $-I(f;g) = \sum P\log_2(Q/P)$ over the support. Apply $\log t \le t-1$ pointwise with $t = Q/P$: the right-hand side telescopes to $(\sum Q - \sum P)/\log 2 = 0$, since both sum to $1$ over the product of the images.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Relabelling invariance]\label{lem:relabel}
If $\varphi$ is injective on $f(S)$ then $H(\varphi\circ f) = H(f)$ and $I(\varphi\circ f;g) = I(f;g)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Factorisation gives completeness]\label{lem:factor}
If $f = \varphi \circ g$ on $S$ then $I(f;g) = H(f)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The joint readout $w \mapsto (\varphi(g(w)),g(w))$ is an injective recoding of $g$, so $H(f,g)=H(g)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Balanced readouts]\label{lem:balanced}
If every nonempty fibre of $f$ has the same cardinality $k>0$, then $H(f) = \log_2 \#f(S)$.
\end{lemma}

\section{The type channel of a Galois group}

\begin{definition}
For $g \in S_n$ the \emph{splitting type} $T(g)$ is the cycle type of $g$. For $n \le 4$ it is faithfully encoded by the pair (number of fixed points, number of points on $2$-cycles).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[The type readout is a class function]\label{lem:classfn}
$T(hgh^{-1}) = T(g)$ for all $g,h$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Conjugation by $h$ carries the fixed-point set of $g$ bijectively onto that of $hgh^{-1}$, and likewise the set of points on $2$-cycles.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Coset readout]
Let $N \trianglelefteq G$. A readout $c : G \to \kappa$ is a \emph{coset readout for $N$} if for all $a,b \in G$,
\[ c(a)=c(b) \iff ab^{-1} \in N. \]
The tautological example is $g \mapsto Ng$; the arithmetically relevant one, with $N = G'$, is the residue class of $p$ modulo $m^{*}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Lagrange, channel form]\label{lem:lagrange}
If $c$ is a coset readout for $N \le G$, every fibre of $c$ is a coset of $N$ and has exactly $\#N$ elements; hence $\#c(G)\cdot\#N = \#G$ and $H(c) = \log_2[G:N]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The fibre over $c(a)$ is $Na$, of size $\#N$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:balanced} with $k=\#N$.
\end{proof}

Throughout the rest of this section, $G$ is a finite group, $N = G'$, $c$ a coset readout for $N$, and $T : G \to \tau$ an arbitrary readout.

\begin{theorem}[The abelianization cap]\label{thm:cap}
For every readout $T$,
\[ 0 \;\le\; I(c;T) \;\le\; \log_2[G:G'] . \]
The bound is independent of the number of values of $T$ and of $H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity is Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbs}; the upper bound is $I(c;T)\le H(c)$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} combined with $H(c) = \log_2[G:G']$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:lagrange}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The law: residue equals abelianization]\label{thm:law}
Let $R : G \to \rho$ factor as $R = \psi \circ c$ with $\psi$ injective on $c(G)$. Then $I(R;T) = I(c;T)$ for every $T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:relabel}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:law} is the formal content of the phrase ``the type channel is the abelianization content'': any dial reading the residue class --- modulo $m^{*}$, modulo any multiple of it, or through any faithful relabelling of the abelian characters --- produces exactly the same number of bits, and no dial can do better.

\begin{proposition}[Decomposition and loss]\label{prop:loss}
For every $c$ and $T$,
\[ I(c;T) = H(T) - H(T\mid c), \qquad \log_2[G:G'] - I(c;T) = H(c \mid T) \;\ge\; 0 . \]
\end{proposition}

The second identity is the ``loss'' column of the tables below: the shortfall from the cap is exactly the residual uncertainty about the coset given the type.

\section{When is a channel complete?}

\begin{theorem}[Determinism criterion]\label{thm:det}
Let $f : S \to A$ and $g : S \to B$ be readouts on a finite uniform sample space. Then
\[ H(g\mid f) = 0 \iff \forall\, w,w' \in S,\ f(w)=f(w') \Rightarrow g(w)=g(w') . \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $P(a,b)$ for the joint probability and $P_1(a)$ for the marginal of $f$. Then
\[
H(g\mid f) = \sum_{a,b}\Big(P(a,b)\log_2 P_1(a) - P(a,b)\log_2 P(a,b)\Big),
\]
the sum over the product of the two images. Each summand is nonnegative: if $P(a,b)=0$ it vanishes, and otherwise $0 < P(a,b)\le P_1(a)$ gives $\log_2 P(a,b) \le \log_2 P_1(a)$. Moreover a summand vanishes \emph{iff} $P(a,b)=0$ or $P(a,b)=P_1(a)$, by strict monotonicity of $\log_2$. So $H(g\mid f)=0$ iff every cell is empty or exhausts its row.

If that holds and $f(w)=f(w')$, the cell $(f(w),g(w))$ is nonempty and hence equals its row, namely the fibre $f^{-1}(f(w)) \ni w'$, so $g(w')=g(w)$. Conversely, if $f$ determines $g$, then every nonempty cell $(a,b)$ satisfies $f^{-1}(a) \subseteq g^{-1}(b)$ and so equals its row, contributing zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:Ieq}
$I(f;g) = H(g)$ if and only if $f$ determines $g$ on $S$. In particular an injective readout $f$ satisfies $I(f;g)=H(g)$ for every $g$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness criterion]\label{thm:complete}
Let $c$ be a coset readout for $G' \le G$ and $T$ any readout. Then
\[
I(c;T) = \log_2[G:G'] \iff \forall\, a,b \in G,\ T(a)=T(b) \Rightarrow c(a)=c(b),
\]
i.e.\ the channel attains the cap exactly when the splitting type determines the abelianization coset.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:loss}, $\log_2[G:G'] - I(c;T) = H(c\mid T)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:det} with $f=T$, $g=c$.
\end{proof}

Completeness is thus a statement about a factorisation of maps, not about entropy: the analytic question ``does this channel run at capacity?'' becomes the finite combinatorial question ``does the cycle-type map factor through the coset map?''.

\begin{theorem}[Abelian fields are complete]\label{thm:abelian}
If $G$ is abelian then $G'=1$, the coset readout is injective, and $I(c;T) = H(T)$ for every readout $T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every commutator is trivial, so $G'=1$ and $c(a)=c(b)$ forces $ab^{-1}=1$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:Ieq}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect groups leak nothing]\label{thm:perfect}
If $G'=G$ then $I(c;T)=0$ for every readout $T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $N=G$ then $ab^{-1}\in N$ always, so $c$ is constant; a constant readout has zero entropy, hence zero mutual information with anything by Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}.
\end{proof}

The smallest nontrivial instance is $G = A_5$: a quintic field with that Galois group has an identically flat type channel, at every level.

\begin{proposition}[Where the deficit comes from]\label{prop:deficit}
If $a,b \in G$ satisfy $c(a)=c(b)$ but $T(a)\ne T(b)$ --- one abelianization coset carrying two distinct types --- then $I(c;T) < H(T)$ strictly.
\end{proposition}

\section{Six fields in closed form: the prime level}\label{sec:prime}

Throughout, $L_3 := \log_2 3 = 1.5849625\ldots$. The groups are realised as permutation groups on the roots:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llcllp{4.6cm}}
\toprule
Field & $G$ & $\#G$ & $G'$ & $G^{\ab}$ & Coset readout \\
\midrule
$x^3\pm x+1$ & $S_3$ & $6$ & $A_3$ & $C_2$ & sign of the permutation \\
$x^4-x-1$ & $S_4$ & $24$ & $A_4$ & $C_2$ & sign of the permutation \\
$x^4+8x+12$ & $A_4$ & $12$ & $V_4$ & $C_3$ & which of the three root-pairings $\{g(0),g(1)\}$ meets \\
$x^4-2$ & $D_4$ & $8$ & centre & $C_2\times C_2$ & sign, plus whether the diagonal $\{0,2\}$ is preserved \\
$x^4-2x^2+9$ & $V_4$ & $4$ & $1$ & $V_4$ & image of a single root \\
$\Phi_5$ & $C_4$ & $4$ & $1$ & $C_4$ & image of a single root \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Here $D_4$ is the stabiliser of the pairing $\{\{0,2\},\{1,3\}\}$ of the roots $\alpha, i\alpha, -\alpha, -i\alpha$ of $x^4-2$, which is exactly the Galois group of that field.

\begin{theorem}[The law table]\label{thm:table}
The six type channels are:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Field & $G$ & $H(T)$ & $I(c;T)$ & cap & loss \\
\midrule
$x^3\pm x+1$ & $S_3$ & $\tfrac23+\tfrac{L_3}{2}\approx 1.45915$ & $1$ & $1$ & $0$ \\
$x^4-x-1$ & $S_4$ & $\tfrac32+\tfrac{3L_3}{8}\approx 2.09436$ & $1$ & $1$ & $0$ \\
$x^4+8x+12$ & $A_4$ & $\tfrac{3L_3}{4}\approx 1.18872$ & $L_3-\tfrac23\approx 0.91830$ & $L_3$ & $\tfrac23$ \\
$x^4-2$ & $D_4$ & $\tfrac52-\tfrac{3L_3}{8}\approx 1.90564$ & $\tfrac94-\tfrac{3L_3}{8}\approx 1.65564$ & $2$ & $\tfrac{3L_3}{8}-\tfrac14$ \\
$x^4-2x^2+9$ & $V_4$ & $2-\tfrac{3L_3}{4}\approx 0.81128$ & $2-\tfrac{3L_3}{4}\approx 0.81128$ & $2$ & $\tfrac{3L_3}{4}$ \\
$\Phi_5$ & $C_4$ & $\tfrac32$ & $\tfrac32$ & $2$ & $\tfrac12$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketches]
\textbf{$S_3$.} The type census is $1,3,2$ out of $6$, giving $H(T) = \tfrac23+\tfrac{L_3}{2}$. The sign is a function of the type ($[1,1,1]$, $[3]$ even; $[2,1]$ odd), so Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} and Lemma~\ref{lem:lagrange} give $I = H(c) = 1$.

\textbf{$S_4$.} The census is $1,6,3,8,6$ for $[1,1,1,1],[2,1,1],[2,2],[3,1],[4]$ out of $24$, giving $H(T) = \tfrac32+\tfrac{3L_3}{8}$. Again the sign is a function of the type, so $I = 1$ exactly. Since the coset $A_4$ carries three types, Proposition~\ref{prop:deficit} gives $I < H(T)$ strictly.

\textbf{$A_4$.} The census is $1,3,8$ for $[1,1,1,1],[2,2],[3,1]$ out of $12$, giving $H(T)=\tfrac{3L_3}{4}$; the coset readout is balanced over three cosets, so $H(c) = L_3$; the identity and the three double transpositions form the trivial coset while the eight $3$-cycles split four-four between the two nontrivial cosets, giving $H(c,T) = \tfrac23 + \tfrac{3L_3}{4}$ and hence $I = L_3-\tfrac23$, loss $\tfrac23$. Structurally, the type $[3,1]$ fills both nontrivial cosets, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} the channel cannot be complete.

\textbf{$D_4$.} The census is $1$ identity, $2$ diagonal reflections of type $[2,1,1]$, $3$ elements of type $[2,2]$, $2$ four-cycles, giving $H(T) = \tfrac52-\tfrac{3L_3}{8}$; $H(c) = 2$ and $H(c,T)=\tfrac94$, whence $I = \tfrac94-\tfrac{3L_3}{8}$. The deficit comes from the three $[2,2]$ elements lying in different cosets.

\textbf{$V_4$, $C_4$.} Abelian, so $I = H(T)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:abelian}; the censuses $1,3$ and $1,1,2$ out of $4$ give $H(T) = 2-\tfrac{3L_3}{4}$ and $H(T) = \tfrac32$ respectively, with caps $2$ and losses $\tfrac{3L_3}{4}$, $\tfrac12$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $S_4$ cap is about the abelianization]
The field $x^4-x-1$ satisfies $I(c;T) = 1 < \tfrac32+\tfrac38 L_3 = H(T)$: a five-type field with more than two bits of splitting entropy leaks exactly the one bit of its quadratic abelianization.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The reversal]\label{cor:reversal}
$I_{V_4} = 2-\tfrac34 L_3 < \tfrac94-\tfrac38 L_3 = I_{D_4}$, and both are strictly below $2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The inequality reduces to $\tfrac38 L_3 > -\tfrac14$; the upper bounds reduce to $L_3 > 1$ and $L_3 > \tfrac23$.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:reversal} refutes the natural expectation that abelian fields carry richer channels. Under a structural ordering by branching of the factorization behaviour the pair $(V_4,D_4)$ is ordered $V_4 > D_4$; the type channels order it the other way. Both orderings are instances of the same law: the fields differ not in the availability of reciprocity but in how well their type readouts separate cosets.

\section{The semiprime level}

\subsection{The model}

A reader presented with a semiprime $N = pq$ sees the residue of $N$, which by multiplicativity of the abelian characters corresponds to the \emph{product} of the two Frobenius cosets, and the \emph{unordered} pair of splitting types $\{T(p),T(q)\}$ --- unordered, because such a reader has no canonical way to attribute a shape to a factor. Modelling the two Frobenius elements as an independent uniform pair $(x,y)\in G\times G$, the semiprime channel is
\[
I_2 := I\big(c(xy);\,\{T(x),T(y)\}\big),
\]
with ordered variant $I_2^{\mathrm{ord}} := I\big(c(xy);\,(T(x),T(y))\big)$.

\begin{theorem}[Semiprime closed forms]\label{thm:semi}
With $L_3=\log_2 3$ and $L_5 = \log_2 5$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
Field & $G$ & $I_2$ & numerically \\
\midrule
$x^3\pm x+1$ & $S_3$ & $1$ & $1.00000$ \\
$x^4-x-1$ & $S_4$ & $1$ & $1.00000$ \\
$x^4+8x+12$ & $A_4$ & $L_3-\tfrac{10}{9}$ & $0.47385$ \\
$x^4-2$ & $D_4$ & $\tfrac{39}{16}-\tfrac34 L_3 + \tfrac{5}{64}L_5$ & $1.43018$ \\
$x^4-2x^2+9$ & $V_4$ & $\tfrac{19}{8}-\tfrac{21}{16}L_3$ & $0.29474$ \\
$\Phi_5$ & $C_4$ & $\tfrac54$ & $1.25000$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In each case the three entropies $H(c(xy))$, $H(\{T(x),T(y)\})$ and the joint entropy are evaluated by census over $G\times G$ (of sizes $36,576,144,64,16,16$). The coset readout of the product is balanced, since $xy$ is uniform on $G$ when $x$ is uniform, so $H(c(xy)) = \log_2[G:G']$ in every row. For $C_4$, for instance, the symmetric type census gives $H = \tfrac{19}{8}$, the joint entropy is $\tfrac{25}{8}$, and $I_2 = 2+\tfrac{19}{8}-\tfrac{25}{8} = \tfrac54$. The $D_4$ row is the only one producing a $\log_2 5$, from a cell of relative weight $5/64$ in the symmetric table.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $C_2$ cap at the semiprime level]
The $S_3$ and $S_4$ fields deliver exactly one bit through a semiprime: a five-type $S_4$ field cannot beat a quadratic one, because the cap is a theorem about the abelianization.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Semiprime reversal]
$I_2(V_4) < I_2(D_4)$ and $I_2(S_4) < I_2(D_4)$: a non-abelian field exceeds one bit and outperforms both the abelian $V_4$ field and every field with quadratic abelianization.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Substituting the closed forms, the claims reduce to $1 < L_3 < 2 < L_5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The which-factor wall]\label{thm:wall}
For each of the six fields,
\[ I\big(c(xy);\,(T(x),T(y))\big) = I\big(c(xy);\,\{T(x),T(y)\}\big). \]
Learning which prime factor carried which splitting shape is worth exactly zero bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides are evaluated by census; for each field the ordered and unordered type entropies differ by exactly the amount by which the corresponding joint entropies differ, so the difference cancels in $I = H(c)+H(\text{type})-H(\text{joint})$. For $C_4$: $H(\mathrm{ord}) = 3$, $H(c,\mathrm{ord}) = \tfrac{15}{4}$, so $I^{\mathrm{ord}} = 2+3-\tfrac{15}{4} = \tfrac54 = I_2$. Structurally, the coset of the product is a symmetric function of $(x,y)$, so the antisymmetric part of the ordered readout is independent of the residue given the multiset.
\end{proof}

\section{Numerical verification}

The closed forms are exact statements about uniform measures on finite groups; their arithmetic relevance rests on Chebotarev equidistribution, an asymptotic statement, so it is worth checking against honest primes.

\paragraph{Prime level.} The splitting type of each unramified prime below a bound is computed by distinct-degree factorization over $\mathbb{F}_p$: iteratively forming $\gcd(x^{p^{d}}-x, f_{\mathrm{rem}})$ for $d=1,2,\ldots$ extracts the product of the degree-$d$ irreducible factors. The residue dial is read from the maximal abelian subextension: the Legendre symbol $\left(\frac{\disc}{p}\right)$ for the $S_3$ and $S_4$ fields, $p \bmod 8$ for $x^4-2$ (maximal abelian subextension $\mathbb{Q}(i,\sqrt2)$, conductor $8$), and $p \bmod 5$ for $\Phi_5$. Over the $6054$ primes below $6\cdot10^{4}$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Field & $G$ & $H(T)$ measured & $I$ measured & law & difference \\
\midrule
$x^3+x+1$ & $S_3$ & $1.45608$ & $1.00000$ & $1.00000$ & $0.00000$ \\
$x^3-x+1$ & $S_3$ & $1.45619$ & $0.99998$ & $1.00000$ & $0.00002$ \\
$x^4-x-1$ & $S_4$ & $2.09112$ & $0.99985$ & $1.00000$ & $0.00015$ \\
$x^4-2$ & $D_4$ & $1.90554$ & $1.65930$ & $1.65564$ & $0.00366$ \\
$\Phi_5$ & $C_4$ & $1.49834$ & $1.49834$ & $1.50000$ & $0.00166$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Agreement is at the level of a few thousandths of a bit, consistent with the $O(1/n)$ upward bias of the plug-in estimator. An independent measurement over roughly $2.3\times10^{4}$ primes per field reproduced the seven prime-level values (counting both $S_3$ fields) as $1.0000$, $1.0000$, $1.0100$, $0.9188$, $1.6555$, $0.8092$, $1.4989$ against the laws $1$, $1$, $1$, $0.91830$, $1.65564$, $0.81128$, $1.5$.

\paragraph{Semiprime level.} Monte Carlo with $4\times10^{5}$ draws per field from unramified pools gives $1.0001$ (both $S_3$ fields), $1.0034$ ($S_4$), $0.4729$ ($A_4$), $1.4325$ ($D_4$), $0.2902$ ($V_4$), $1.2461$ ($C_4$) against the laws $1$, $1$, $1$, $0.47385$, $1.43018$, $0.29474$, $1.25$; the which-factor walls measure $0.0000$--$0.0001$ bits.

\paragraph{Two estimator traps.} (i) \emph{Sparse-modulus bias}: a plug-in mutual-information estimate over a table with many cells is biased upward by roughly (number of free cells)$/(2n\ln 2)$; for the several-hundred-cell $S_4$ semiprime table a $3\times10^{4}$-draw estimate would carry a bias near $0.10$ bits, enough to fake a violation of the one-bit cap. Raising the draw count to $4\times10^{5}$ puts the bias below the $0.005$-bit resolution of the claims, and permutation nulls confirm residual thickening at the $0.004$-bit level. (ii) \emph{The resolvent shortcut}: reading quartic types from the cubic resolvent is invalid for binomial and other special quartics --- the axis pairing of $x^4-2$ is fixed by all of $D_4$, and for the $V_4$ field the resolvent splits over $\mathbb{Q}$. Types must be computed by genuine factor-degree extraction over $\mathbb{F}_p$, equivalently by counting roots in $\mathbb{F}_p$ and $\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$.

\section{Discussion and applications}

\subsection{A capacity for arithmetic side channels}

Theorem~A is a budget. If a protocol exposes, per observation, the factorization shape of a fixed polynomial at a prime associated with a secret, the adversary's per-observation yield about the residue class is exactly $I = H(T)-H(T\mid\text{coset}) \le \log_2[G:G']$, computable from a group table of size $\#G$ in time $O(\#G)$. Three design consequences follow: a polynomial with perfect Galois group leaks nothing at all; a polynomial with $G^{\ab}=C_2$ leaks at most one bit per observation regardless of the intricacy of its statistics; and no residue dial that resolves the abelianization is better than any other (Theorem~\ref{thm:law}). Aggregation across the factors of a semiprime does not escape the bound, though a field with a larger abelianization genuinely offers more.

\subsection{A quantitative frontier for reciprocity}

The abelian/non-abelian divide is classically a dichotomy; the law replaces it by a real-valued coordinate:
\[
\underbrace{0}_{\text{perfect } G,\ \text{e.g. } A_5} \;\le\; I \;\le\; \underbrace{H(T)}_{\text{abelian } G}, \qquad I \le \log_2[G:G'] .
\]
Which bound binds is structural: $I = H(T)$ iff the coset determines the type, while $I = \log_2[G:G']$ iff the type determines the coset. The six fields realise three of the four combinations: $S_3$ and $S_4$ attain the cap but not the type entropy; $V_4$ and $C_4$ the type entropy but not the cap; $A_4$ and $D_4$ neither.

\subsection{Unification}

Flatness statements for certain residue channels, criteria for a channel to be nonzero, binary laws for quadratic fields, tabulations for small groups and abelian pair laws at the semiprime level are all projections of Theorem~A. Once the channel is known to equal the abelianization content, each reduces to arithmetic on a finite group table.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{The index-2 rigidity conjecture.} Every field in the table with abelianization $C_2$ attains its cap exactly, while every field with a larger abelianization falls short. For a subgroup of index $2$ the coset readout is the sign of the permutation, and the sign is a function of the cycle type. \emph{Conjecture:} if $[G:G']=2$ and the type readout is the cycle type of a faithful transitive action, then $I=1$ exactly; more generally the channel is complete iff the coset map factors through the cycle-type map, which for a transitive degree-$n$ action happens for every $G$ with $G' \supseteq G\cap A_n$. The completeness criterion has already converted the analytic question into this combinatorial one; what remains needs only $\sign(g) = (-1)^{\,n-\#\text{cycles}(g)}$.

\paragraph{Perfect-group flatness as a theorem about $A_5$.} The general statement is proved; its smallest instance predicts an identically zero channel for an $A_5$ quintic field, for primes and semiprimes alike. Flatness needs no entropy computation: perfection collapses the coset readout to a constant. What remains is the certificate of perfection, most cleanly obtained from the simplicity of $A_5$ rather than from a $60\times60$ commutator census.

\paragraph{The $k$-almost-prime ladder.} The prime and semiprime layers are $k=1,2$ of
\[ I_k = I\big(\text{coset}(g_1\cdots g_k);\ \text{multiset of types}\big). \]
\emph{Conjecture:} $I_k$ is non-increasing in $k$ and tends to $0$ for every field whose abelianization is not an elementary abelian $2$-group, while for $G^{\ab}$ of exponent $2$ it stabilises at a positive limit --- for $S_3$ and $S_4$ at the constant one bit.

\paragraph{Beyond.} Extending the closed forms to degree $5$ and $6$, where the type readout is no longer determined by fixed-point counts; characterising which pairs (group, action) realise a prescribed loss; and studying the channel under conditioning on additional arithmetic data, where the cap still applies but the completeness criterion changes.

\section{Conclusion}

The complete splitting-type channel of a number field is exactly its abelianization content:
\[
I\big(p\bmod m^{*};\,T\big) = I(\text{coset};\,T) = H(T)-H(T\mid\text{coset}) \le \log_2[G:G'],
\]
with equality precisely when the splitting type determines the abelianization coset. At the semiprime level the same law holds verbatim with the class-level type map, and telling the reader which factor carried which shape is worth exactly nothing. Six fields --- $S_3$ twice, $S_4$, $A_4$, $D_4$, with abelian controls $V_4$ and $C_4$ --- are evaluated in exact closed form at both levels, and measurements over real primes reproduce every value. The $S_4$ field shows that the cap sees only the abelianization and not the five splitting types; the $D_4$/$V_4$ pair shows that a non-abelian channel can be strictly richer than an abelian one; and perfect groups, of which $A_5$ is the smallest, are exactly and permanently silent.

\end{document}

